{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Beautiful of form.</b> Rashi's comment: even a married woman. And if you ask, whence do we derive this midrash? One may answer: we do not find the term אשת for one who is not married, and it could have written אשה יפת תואר. Now that it writes <b>אשת</b>, this teaches the midrash that it refers to אשת איש (a married woman). This is what it says: since she is a married woman, she would be forbidden to you even after all the procedures required of you to perform upon her — because the heart is drawn after her husband, and she is intimate with his winks and his hints, and there is reason for concern. It comes to teach that even so, she is permitted." "<b>And you shall take.</b> According to its plain sense: <b>and you desire her, and you shall take</b> her <b>for yourself as a wife</b> — as it is written above: 'only the women and the little ones you shall take as spoil for yourself.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The abbreviations וא״ת, וה״‎ל, ש״‎מ, סד״‎א, ואיכא למיחש, קמ״ל are resolved as 'and if you ask', 'and it could have', 'this teaches', 'this is what it says', 'and there is reason for concern', 'it comes to teach' respectively."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:39.285933274Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}